<% content_for :title, "Hinduism | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="spirituality-hinduism-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="spirituality-hinduism-hero">
    <div class="spirituality-hinduism-hero-content">

      <p class="spirituality-hinduism-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / SPIRITUALITY / HINDUISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>YOU ARE</span>
        <span>MORE THAN</span>
        <span>THIS MOMENT.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="spirituality-hinduism-intro">
        SELF. DHARMA. KARMA. YOGA.<br>
        ACTION WITHOUT CLINGING TO THE OUTCOME.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="spirituality-hinduism-content">

    <!-- 01 / INTRODUCTION -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-black">

      <p class="sh-number">01 / THE BIG FUCKING PICTURE</p>

      <h2>
        THERE IS<br>
        MORE TO<br>
        EXPLORE.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          In Hinduism, you encounter a vast family of traditions,
          philosophies, sacred texts, rituals, and spiritual
          practices. You will find different understandings
          of the divine, the Self, and ultimate reality.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can explore spiritual life through knowledge,
          ethical action, meditation, service, and devotion.
          Different traditions place different emphasis
          on these paths and where they lead.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-stack">
          <span>WHO ARE YOU?</span>
          <span>HOW WILL YOU LIVE?</span>
          <span>WHAT ARE YOUR ACTIONS CREATING?</span>
          <span>WHAT DO YOU HOLD ONTO?</span>
          <span>WHAT WOULD FREEDOM MEAN?</span>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 02 / BRAHMAN AND ATMAN -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-pink">

      <p class="sh-number">02 / BRAHMAN &amp; ATMAN</p>

      <h2>
        WHO THE<br>
        FUCK<br>
        ARE YOU?
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Your thoughts, emotions, body, and circumstances change.
          Through the teachings of the Upanishads, you can
          investigate the nature of the Self and its relationship
          with ultimate reality.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-grid">

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>BRAHMAN</span>
            <h3>ULTIMATE REALITY</h3>
            <p>
              You encounter Brahman as the ultimate reality
              underlying existence, understood differently
              across Hindu philosophical schools.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>ATMAN</span>
            <h3>THE INNERMOST SELF</h3>
            <p>
              You investigate a Self distinct from your
              changing body, thoughts, emotions,
              and social identities.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          In Advaita Vedanta, you explore the teaching that
          Atman and Brahman are ultimately identical.
          In other traditions, you encounter qualified unity
          or an enduring distinction between the individual
          soul and the divine.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-callout">
          WHAT DO YOU MEAN<br>
          WHEN YOU SAY<br>
          "YOUR SELF"?
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 03 / IGNORANCE AND IDENTITY -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-yellow">

      <p class="sh-number">03 / AVIDYA</p>

      <h2>
        LOOK BEYOND<br>
        THE LABELS<br>
        YOU CARRY.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Avidya refers to ignorance or misapprehension,
          especially concerning the nature of the Self
          and reality.
        </p>

        <p>
          You may identify so completely with your achievements,
          possessions, appearance, or emotions that any change
          feels like a threat to your entire existence.
          Your sense of worth becomes tied to conditions
          you cannot permanently preserve.
        </p>

        <p>
          Self-inquiry invites you to examine those identifications.
          What are you taking to be your whole identity?
          What changes when you look more closely?
        </p>

        <div class="sh-thought">
          HOW MUCH OF YOUR IDENTITY<br>
          DEPENDS ON SOMETHING<br>
          THAT CAN CHANGE?
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 04 / DHARMA -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-purple">

      <p class="sh-number">04 / DHARMA</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT IS<br>
        YOURS TO<br>
        FUCKING DO?
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Through dharma, you consider right conduct, duty,
          responsibility, and your relationship with the
          order that sustains life.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your circumstances and obligations matter.
          You examine what honesty, compassion, and integrity
          ask of you in the situation you actually face.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-stack">
          <span>WHAT RESPONSIBILITIES DO YOU HAVE?</span>
          <span>WHO WILL YOUR DECISION AFFECT?</span>
          <span>WHAT DOES HONESTY REQUIRE?</span>
          <span>WHAT ACTION CAN YOU STAND BEHIND?</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          You may need to act while feeling uncertain,
          disappointed, or afraid. Your immediate preferences
          are part of the decision, alongside the commitments
          you want to honor.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 05 / KARMA -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-black">

      <p class="sh-number">05 / KARMA</p>

      <h2>
        YOUR ACTIONS<br>
        LEAVE<br>
        A TRACE.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Karma concerns action and its consequences,
          including consequences understood across lifetimes
          in many Hindu traditions.
        </p>

        <p>
          In everyday reflection, you can examine how your
          intentions and actions influence your habits,
          relationships, and the circumstances that follow.
          What you repeatedly do helps shape your direction.
        </p>

        <p>
          You cannot infer someone's moral worth from their
          suffering. Treating illness or hardship as deserved
          punishment overlooks the complexity of their life
          and can become an excuse to withhold care.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-callout">
          CONSIDER YOUR INTENTIONS.<br>
          EXAMINE YOUR ACTIONS.<br>
          ATTEND TO THEIR CONSEQUENCES.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 06 / THE GITA AND ACTION -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-pink">

      <p class="sh-number">06 / THE BHAGAVAD GITA</p>

      <h2>
        DO THE WORK.<br>
        LOOSEN YOUR<br>
        FUCKING GRIP.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          In the Bhagavad Gita, you encounter a dialogue
          between Krishna and Arjuna, a warrior overwhelmed
          by a moral crisis.
        </p>

        <p>
          Through their exchange, you explore duty, spiritual
          knowledge, devotion, and action without attachment
          to its fruits.
        </p>

        <p>
          Applied to your life, this invites you to act sincerely
          while recognizing that your effort cannot guarantee
          a particular result.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-grid">

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>YOUR PARTICIPATION</span>
            <h3>ATTEND TO THE ACTION.</h3>
            <p>
              Prepare carefully. Examine your intentions.
              Act honestly. Consider consequences.
              Follow through where you can.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>YOUR ATTACHMENT</span>
            <h3>RELEASE THE DEMAND.</h3>
            <p>
              Notice when you require success, recognition,
              or a specific response before your effort
              can feel worthwhile.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          Through karma yoga, you practice action as service
          and spiritual discipline. You remain attentive
          to consequences while loosening your dependence
          on personal reward.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 07 / PATHS OF YOGA -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-yellow">

      <p class="sh-number">07 / PATHS OF YOGA</p>

      <h2>
        MORE THAN<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        STRETCHING.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          You can approach yoga as spiritual discipline,
          with paths involving action, devotion, knowledge,
          and meditation. These approaches can complement
          one another within your practice.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-grid">

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>01 / ACTION</span>
            <h3>KARMA YOGA</h3>
            <p>
              Serve and fulfill your responsibilities
              while releasing attachment to personal reward.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>02 / DEVOTION</span>
            <h3>BHAKTI YOGA</h3>
            <p>
              Cultivate love and relationship with the divine
              through prayer, worship, chanting, and offerings.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>03 / KNOWLEDGE</span>
            <h3>JNANA YOGA</h3>
            <p>
              Study, contemplate, and inquire into
              the nature of the Self and reality.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>04 / MEDITATIVE DISCIPLINE</span>
            <h3>RAJA YOGA</h3>
            <p>
              Develop ethical discipline, concentration,
              and meditative awareness.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          You might study a teaching, offer a prayer,
          help someone, and spend time in meditation.
          Your practice can involve several ways
          of learning and participating.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 08 / GUNAS -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-purple">

      <p class="sh-number">08 / THE THREE GUNAS</p>

      <h2>
        NOTICE THE<br>
        QUALITIES<br>
        AT WORK.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          In Samkhya and related Hindu teachings,
          you encounter three gunas: qualities of nature
          that combine in changing proportions.
          You can use this framework to contemplate
          the qualities influencing your experience.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-grid">

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>SATTVA</span>
            <h3>CLARITY &amp; HARMONY</h3>
            <p>
              Notice qualities of illumination,
              balance, understanding, and calm.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>RAJAS</span>
            <h3>ACTIVITY &amp; PASSION</h3>
            <p>
              Notice movement, striving, desire,
              restlessness, and agitation.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>TAMAS</span>
            <h3>INERTIA &amp; OBSCURITY</h3>
            <p>
              Notice heaviness, dullness,
              resistance, and lack of clarity.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          You cultivate sattva to support discernment
          and spiritual understanding. In the Gita,
          liberation ultimately involves going beyond
          identification with all three gunas.
        </p>

        <p>
          Use these as philosophical concepts.
          Your bipolar symptoms require their own
          clinical understanding and care.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 09 / NON-HARMING -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-black">

      <p class="sh-number">09 / AHIMSA</p>

      <h2>
        PRACTICE<br>
        WITHOUT<br>
        CRUELTY.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Through ahimsa, or non-harming, you consider
          how your conduct affects yourself and other beings.
          Your words, intentions, and actions deserve attention.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can practice restraint, maintain boundaries,
          and take responsibility while respecting your
          own dignity. Discipline can develop through
          patient, consistent care.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-stack">
          <span>CONSIDER THE HARM YOUR ACTION COULD CAUSE.</span>
          <span>SPEAK WITH HONESTY AND CARE.</span>
          <span>RESPECT YOUR LIMITS AND OTHERS' BOUNDARIES.</span>
          <span>MAKE REPAIRS WHERE YOU CAN.</span>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 10 / DETACHMENT -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-pink">

      <p class="sh-number">10 / VAIRAGYA &amp; EQUANIMITY</p>

      <h2>
        CARE DEEPLY.<br>
        HOLD<br>
        MORE LIGHTLY.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Through vairagya, you cultivate dispassion
          toward the objects and rewards that keep
          pulling your attention outward.
        </p>

        <p>
          In your daily life, you can examine where love
          becomes possessiveness, effort becomes obsession,
          or enjoyment becomes a demand for more.
        </p>

        <p>
          Equanimity develops as you learn to meet success
          and failure, pleasure and pain, with greater
          steadiness. You can remain committed while
          allowing circumstances to change.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-callout">
          YOU CAN CARE WITHOUT OWNING.<br>
          YOU CAN TRY WITHOUT GUARANTEEING.<br>
          YOU CAN ACT AMID UNCERTAINTY.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 11 / LIBERATION -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-yellow">

      <p class="sh-number">11 / SAMSARA &amp; MOKSHA</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DOES<br>
        FREEDOM<br>
        MEAN TO YOU?
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          In many Hindu traditions, you encounter samsara
          as the cycle of birth, death, and rebirth,
          connected with karma, attachment, and ignorance.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-grid">

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>SAMSARA</span>
            <h3>CONTINUED BONDAGE</h3>
            <p>
              Explore how karma and ignorance bind
              the individual to repeated birth and death.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sh-mini-card">
            <span>MOKSHA</span>
            <h3>LIBERATION</h3>
            <p>
              Explore freedom from samsara, understood
              through self-realization or relationship
              with the divine in different traditions.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          Your understanding of liberation will depend
          on the tradition you study. You may encounter
          realization of nonduality, enduring communion
          with the divine, or other accounts of spiritual freedom.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 12 / PRACTICE -->
    <article class="sh-section sh-section-purple">

      <p class="sh-number">12 / EVERYDAY PRACTICE</p>

      <h2>
        BRING IT<br>
        INTO YOUR<br>
        ACTUAL LIFE.
      </h2>

      <div class="sh-copy">

        <p>
          Give your spiritual practice a place in your
          responsibilities, relationships, study,
          devotion, and quiet moments.
        </p>

        <div class="sh-practice-grid">

          <div>
            <span>01</span>
            <p>
              Ask what integrity requires
              in the situation in front of you.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>02</span>
            <p>
              Separate the effort you can offer
              from the outcome you hope to receive.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>03</span>
            <p>
              Notice an identity, possession, or expectation
              you are holding tightly.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>04</span>
            <p>
              Make room for prayer, an offering,
              contemplation, or meditation.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>05</span>
            <p>
              Practice ahimsa through one deliberate
              act of care or restraint.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>06</span>
            <p>
              Offer service without requiring
              recognition or repayment.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          Let your practice develop through repetition,
          reflection, and learning. You can keep asking
          deeper questions while attending to
          the responsibilities of your life.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- ENDING -->
    <article class="sh-ending">

      <p>
        YOUR INTENTIONS.<br>
        YOUR PARTICIPATION.<br>
        YOUR PRACTICE.
      </p>

      <h2>
        DO YOUR PART.<br>
        LOOSEN<br>
        YOUR GRIP.
      </h2>

      <p class="sh-ending-copy">
        Act with integrity, cultivate understanding,
        and make room for what you cannot determine.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO SPIRITUALITY",
          spirituality_path,
          class: "sh-back-link" %>

    </article>

  </section>

</main>